## Why This Is Asked

Tests advanced fairness and isolation in a multi-tenant KCNA cluster; demonstrates practical reliability under bursty traffic.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant rate limiting (token bucket, leaky bucket)
- Global throughput vs. per-tenant isolation
- Distributed state store for buckets (Redis, etc.)
- Backpressure mechanisms and producer signaling
- Canary strategy and measurable success criteria

## Code Example

```javascript
// simplified token bucket sketch
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(rate, capacity) {
    this.rate = rate;
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.tokens = capacity;
    this.lastRefill = Date.now();
  }

  allow(n = 1) {
    this.refill();
    if (this.tokens >= n) {
      this.tokens -= n;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }

  refill() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.lastRefill) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + elapsed * this.rate);
    this.lastRefill = now;
  }
}

// Global limiter wrapping per-tenant buckets
class TenantRateController {
  constructor(tenants, globalRate) {
    this.buckets = new Map();
    this.globalLimiter = new TokenBucket(globalRate, globalRate * 2);
    for (const [id, quota] of tenants) {
      this.buckets.set(id, new TokenBucket(quota.rate, quota.burst));
    }
  }

  tryProcess(tenantId, n = 1) {
    const bucket = this.buckets.get(tenantId);
    if (!bucket || !bucket.allow(n)) return false;
    if (!this.globalLimiter.allow(n)) {
      bucket.tokens += n; // rollback tenant token
      return false;
    }
    return true;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Auditors require end-to-end data provenance in KCNA with tenant isolation, cryptographic integrity, and scalable verification. This question tests how to design lightweight, verifiable lineage without payload leakage and how to handle dynamic rotation and maintenance under failure.

## Key Concepts

- Data provenance and per-tenant isolation
- Cryptographic hash chaining and Merkle trees
- Envelope encryption and retention policies
- At-least-once semantics and failure recovery

## Code Example

```javascript
// Lightweight Merkle root builder (conceptual)
class MerkleTree {
  constructor(){ this.leaves = []; this.rootHash = null; }
  addLeaf(data) { this.leaves.push(hash(data)); this.rootHash = this.computeRoot(); }
  computeRoot(){ // simplistic binary Merkle root placeholder
    let nodes = this.leaves.slice();
    while (nodes.length > 1) {
      const next = [];
      for (let i = 0; i < nodes.length; i += 2) {
        const a = nodes[i];
        const b = nodes[i+1] || a;
        next.push(hash(a + b));
      }
      nodes = next;
    }
    return nodes[0] || null;
  }
}
function hash(x){ // placeholder for a real crypto hash
  return require('crypto').createHash('sha256').update(x).digest('hex');
}
```

