## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a stateful, TTL-based enrichment with side-input updates, late-data handling, and idempotent sinks in KCNA. Reflects real-world needs for per-user enrichment at scale.

## Key Concepts
- TTL-based in-memory caches for streaming enrichment
- Side-input stream for cache population
- Exactly-once vs at-least-once semantics in sinks
- Re-enrichment triggers and idempotent downstream writes
- Offset management across multi-topic joins

## Code Example
```javascript
// Skeleton enrichment logic
class Enricher {
  constructor(ttlMs) {
    this.cache = new TTLCache(ttlMs); // maps userId -> segment
  }
  enrichMessage(clickEvent) {
    const seg = this.cache.get(clickEvent.userId);
    if (seg) return { ...clickEvent, segment: seg };
    // emit with unknown and schedule re-enrichment on cache update
    return { ...clickEvent, segment: 'unknown' };
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test eviction and re-enrichment without data loss?
- How would you scale the TTL cache across multiple workers?
- How would you detect and recover from cache corruption or delays in side-inputs?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests per-key stateful processing, TTL-based windowing, and idempotence across replays—core KCNA concepts—while exercising cross-event aggregation and emission semantics.

## Key Concepts
- Per-key in-memory or stateful session store with a 30-second TTL
- Idempotent emits using a deterministic sessionId (userId:start)
- Out-of-order arrivals handled via lastEventTs and session boundaries
- At-least-once delivery guaranteed by committing offsets only after emit

## Code Example
```javascript
function onMessage(ctx, msg) {
  const session = sessionStore().getOrCreate(msg.userId, () => ({
    startTs: msg.timestamp,
    endTs: msg.timestamp,
    count: 0,
    lastEventTs: msg.timestamp
  }));
  session.endTs = msg.timestamp;
  session.lastEventTs = msg.timestamp;
  session.count++;
  sessionStore().setTtl(msg.userId, 30000, () => {
    ctx.emit('sessions', {
      userId: msg.userId,
      sessionStart: session.startTs,
      sessionEnd: session.endTs,
      eventCount: session.count
    });
    sessionStore().remove(msg.userId);
  });
}

function commitOffsets() {
  ctx.commit();
}

function sessionStore() {
  return ctx.state();
}
```

